Working with columns: **select()**
:::

## **select()** verb

When working with data we will find different columns and plenty of variables, and sometimes we will only want to work with some of them.

We can use `select()` to subset some of them for our analysis and avoid using the full data set.

```{r}
library(gapminder)
```

## Selecting a single column

::: {style="font-size:30px;"}
We want to analyze only the variable related to life expectancy (**lifeExp**):

::: {layout-ncol="2"}
```{r,echo=TRUE}
gapminder %>% 
  select(lifeExp)
```

-   We pass the data frame to the function using the pipe operator (`%>%`) to select function.

-   The argument is the variable with out commas: `lifeExp`.

-   This will return a data frame of 1074 rows and 1 column.
:::
:::

## Selecting multiple columns

::: {style="font-size:30px;"}
::: {layout-ncol="2"}
```{r, echo=TRUE}
#| code-line-numbers: "2" 
gapminder %>% 
  select(lifeExp, country)
```

```{r, echo=TRUE}
#| code-line-numbers: "2" 
gapminder %>% 
  select(lifeExp:country)
```
:::

Take into account that the function doesn't modify the data frame, if you want to keep it you need to save it in a new data frame

```{r, echo=TRUE}
gapminder2 <- gapminder %>% 
  select(lifeExp:country)
```
:::

## Inverse selection (all except)

::: {layout-ncol="2"}
```{r, echo=TRUE}
#| code-line-numbers: "2" 
gapminder %>% 
  select(-lifeExp, -pop, -year)
```

```{r, echo=TRUE}
#| code-line-numbers: "2" 
gapminder %>% 
  select(-(lifeExp:country))
```
:::

## Advanced selection

::: {style="font-size:30px"}
There are some special functions we can use together with `select()`

-   `starts_with("abc")` will select all columns that start with `abc`
-   `ends_with("abc")` will select all columns that end with `abc`
-   `contains("abc")` column that contains `abc`
-   `matches("(.)//1")` use regular expression to select the column
-   `num_range("x",1:3)` will select x1, x2 and x3
:::

## Advanced selection

An example...

```{r, echo=TRUE}
gapminder %>% 
  select(starts_with("co"))
```

## Select `everything()`

Useful to put some columns first and then the rest...

```{r, echo=TRUE}
gapminder %>% 
  select(year, everything())
```

