## Filtering

For building complex filters we need to know the different logical operators...


::: {style="font-size: 30px;"}
::: {layout-ncol="2"} 


| operator | definition|
|-----------|----------|
| <  | Less than |
| <= | Less or equal than |
| >  | More than   |
| >= | More or equal than |
| == | Equal |
| != | Distinct |



| operator | definition|
|-----------|----------|
| x & y  | x AND y |
| x \| y  | x OR y |
| is.na(x) | is x a NA value |
| x %in% y | x belongs to y |
| !x | opposite of x * |

:::

*You can use `!` to negate an expression meaning the opposite
:::

## **filter()** verb

::: {style="font-size: 30px;"}
All the multiple arguments we provide, are analyzed as a single filtering condition. 

Example: American countries that had life expectancy superior than 75 years or GDP inferior to 6000 during 2007. 
:::

```{r, echo=TRUE, eval=FALSE}
#| code-line-numbers: "2,3" 
gapminder %>% 
  filter(year == 2007, continent == "Americas",
         (lifeExp > 77 | gdpPercap < 6000))
```

```{r}
gapminder %>% 
  filter(year == 2007, continent == "Americas",
         (lifeExp > 77 | gdpPercap < 6000)) %>% head(10)
```

