## **mutate()** verb

We can create new columns...

```{r, echo=TRUE}
gapminder %>%
  mutate(gdp = gdpPercap * pop) %>%
  filter(year == 2007) %>%
  arrange( desc(gdp) )
```


## **mutate()** verb
Some considerations...

*   You can create more than one variable in a single call to the function. Each variable will be in a different argument. 
*   You can re-use a variable (it will be overwritten).

## Creating new columns
Another multiple functions you can use to create columns...

* Arithmetic operators.
* Modular arithmetic operators: integer division `%/%` and module `%%`
* Logarithm
* Cumulative aggregations: `cumsum()`, `cumprod()`, `cummin()` and `cummax()`
* Logical comparisons
* Rankings: `min rank()`

## Creating columns

Some examples...

```{r, echo=TRUE}
x = c(1,3,2,7,6)
# Ranking
min_rank(x)

# Module
33 %% 10

# Cumulative sum
cumsum(x)
```


