## Grouping data
::: {style="font-size:30px"}
**summary()** function is powerful when creating groups with **group_by()**. We can obtain different summary stats for groups in the data. 
:::

```{r, echo=TRUE}
gapminder %>%
  filter(year == 1967) %>%
  group_by(continent) %>%
  summarise(mean_gdpPerCapita = mean(gdpPercap))
```
::: {style="font-size:30px"}
Take into account that ```group_by()``` creates groups but doesn't aggregates the data, that's why you need `summarise()`
:::
## Grouping data


```{r, echo=TRUE}
gapminder %>%
  filter(year == 1967) %>%
  group_by(continent) %>%
  summarise(n = n(),
            mean_gdpPerCapita = mean(gdpPercap),
            median_gdpPercapita = median(gdpPercap),
            )
```

::: {style="font-size:30px"}
**Missing values.** Take into account that missing values can be present on the data. Use `na.rm = TRUE` e.g. `mean(x, na.rm=TRUE)`

:::

## Grouping data
::: {style="font-size:30px"}
Grouping more variables...
:::

```{r, echo=TRUE, eval=FALSE}
gapminder %>%
  group_by(continent, year) %>%
  summarise(
            max_gdpPercapita    = max(gdpPercap),
            min_gdpPercapita    = min(gdpPercap)
            ) %>% 
  filter(year %in% c(1967, 2007))
```

```{r}
gapminder %>%
  group_by(continent, year) %>%
  summarise(
            max_gdpPercapita    = max(gdpPercap),
            min_gdpPercapita    = min(gdpPercap)
            ) %>% 
  filter(year %in% c(1967, 2007)) %>% 
  head(8)
```

## Grouping data, another approach
::: {style="font-size:30px"}
I want for each country to have the deviation from the continents maximum life expectancy for each year. 
:::

```{r, echo=TRUE, eval=FALSE}
gapminder %>% 
  group_by(year, continent) %>% 
  mutate(
    lifeExp_dev = lifeExp - max(lifeExp)
  ) %>% 
  ungroup() # important to avoid unexpected behaviors
```

```{r}
gapminder %>% 
  group_by(year, continent) %>% 
  mutate(
    lifeExp_dev = lifeExp - max(lifeExp)
  ) %>% 
  arrange(year, continent, desc(lifeExp)) %>% 
  head(5)
```

